    kfree(ft->g);
    kvfree(in);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because if the allocation of `in` fails after `ft->g` has already been allocated, the function attempts to free `ft->g` and then returns an error. This leads to a situation where the caller of `arfs_create_groups` will subsequently call `mlx5e_destroy_flow_table`, which tries to free `ft->g` again, resulting in a double-free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (!ft->g)
    return -ENOMEM;

in = kvzalloc(inlen, GFP_KERNEL);
if (!in) {
    err = -ENOMEM;
    goto err_free_g;
}
```
Reason:
The fix ensures that if `ft->g` is not allocated successfully, the function returns an error immediately without proceeding to allocate `in`. This prevents the double-free scenario by ensuring that `ft->g` is only freed in the error handling section if it was successfully allocated, thus avoiding the situation where it could be freed multiple times.

